  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "cil_gen_validatetrans",
    "file": "selinux/libsepol/cil/src/cil_build_ast.c",
    "description": "`cil_gen_validatetrans` stores an expression list in `validtrans->str_expr` via `cil_gen_constraint_expr(...)` but does not ensure ownership/uniqueness of list elements for later destruction. During teardown, `cil_destroy_validatetrans` calls `cil_list_destroy(&validtrans->str_expr, CIL_TRUE)`, which recursively calls `cil_destroy_data` on list item payloads that are not safely heap-owned for this path (or are reused), leading to invalid memory write when `cil_destroy_data` does `*data = NULL` (crash at cil.c:1016). Root cause is incorrect construction/ownership semantics in validatetrans AST generation, not the generic destroy helper itself.",
    "conditions": "Trigger with a malformed `validatetrans` constraint expression that still builds enough AST to reach DB destruction, e.g. fuzz input like `(validatetrans f(eq t3(P)))`. Data flows from fuzzer input -> CIL parser -> `cil_gen_validatetrans`/`cil_gen_constraint_expr` producing `validtrans->str_expr` entries -> cleanup path (`cil_db_destroy` -> tree destroy -> `cil_destroy_validatetrans` -> `cil_list_destroy(..., CIL_TRUE)` -> `cil_destroy_data`). Crash requires list items whose `data` pointers are not valid writable owned objects for `cil_destroy_data` nulling/free semantics."
  },
